The two `acme` rows share a placement, as do the two `globex` rows; compare `colocation_id` values rather than expecting specific numbers, and expect the worker assignment to vary between runs.

Schema-based sharding is useful when each tenant is large or needs schema customization because one tenant can be moved as a unit while retaining ordinary joins and foreign keys inside its schema. Shared row-based tables are usually better for thousands of small tenants because they avoid creating and maintaining thousands of PostgreSQL schemas.

Use the same distribution column and colocation group on every tenant-owned table. Composite keys then align database constraints with Citus routing.

